window.VEREDA_CONTENT={"modules": [{"id": 1, "title": "Rooted & Unshakable", "subtitle": "Build a faith that stands firm in every season", "chapters": [{"title": "Rooted in Faith", "lessons": ["A Lamp for Your Path", "Called by Name", "Loved Before You Earned It", "Pray Without Pretending", "Seasons of Waiting", "Faith Over Fear", "Review: Roots Go Deep"]}, {"title": "Walking With Jesus", "lessons": ["The Invitation to Follow", "Dinner With Sinners", "The Quiet Mountain", "Peace in the Storm", "Bread for the Hungry", "Washing Feet", "Review: Following Closely"]}, {"title": "The Power of Prayer", "lessons": ["Our Father", "Ask, Seek, Knock", "When Heaven Feels Silent", "Praying the Psalms", "Standing in the Gap", "Gratitude That Changes You", "Review: A Praying Life"]}, {"title": "Freedom From Worry", "lessons": ["Birds and Lilies", "Cast Your Cares", "Tomorrow Belongs to God", "Enough for Today", "Rest for the Weary", "Contentment Learned", "Review: Unhurried Trust"]}, {"title": "Hearing God Every Day", "lessons": ["The Still Small Voice", "Scripture That Speaks", "Wise Counsel", "Open and Closed Doors", "Peace as a Guide", "Obeying the Nudge", "Review: Listening Hearts"]}, {"title": "Habits of the Heart", "lessons": ["Morning With God", "Fasting and Focus", "Sabbath Rest", "Generous Hands", "Honest Confession", "Worship in Ordinary Days", "Review: Small Faithful Steps"]}, {"title": "People Who Trusted God", "lessons": ["Noah Builds Anyway", "Hannah Pours Out Her Heart", "Gideon’s Small Army", "Naomi and Ruth", "Daniel’s Window", "Mary Says Yes", "Review: A Great Cloud of Witnesses"]}]}, {"id": 2, "title": "Love That Builds", "subtitle": "Strengthen your relationships with grace and truth", "chapters": []}], "lessons": {"1-1-1": {"image": "lamp", "prayer": ["Heavenly Father,", "Thank you for this new beginning. I come to you as I am — with my questions, my hopes, and the things I carry quietly.", "You promised that your Word would be a lamp for my feet. I don’t need to see the whole road today; I only need enough light for the next step. Give me that light.", "Quiet the noise around me and inside me. Open my eyes to see you in the ordinary moments of this day, and give me a heart that wants to follow you.", "In Jesus’ name, Amen."], "devotional": [{"h": "Enough Light for the Next Step"}, {"p": "Picture a hiker on a mountain trail an hour before sunrise. The path is steep, the air is cold, and the only light she has comes from a small lamp strapped to her forehead. It doesn’t light up the whole mountain. It doesn’t show her the summit, the switchbacks ahead, or the view waiting at the top. It shows about three steps — **and three steps are enough to keep walking.**"}, {"p": "Most of us wish God worked differently. We want the whole map: where our career is heading, how a relationship will turn out, whether the diagnosis will change, what the next five years will look like. We tell ourselves that if we could just *see* it all, we would finally feel at peace. But that is rarely how God leads His people. Again and again in Scripture, **God gives light for the next step, not for the entire journey.**"}, {"p": "Abraham was told to go to “the land I will show you” — without being told where it was. Israel followed a cloud by day and fire by night, one campsite at a time. The disciples were simply told, “Follow me.” None of them received a detailed plan. What they received was a Person to follow and enough light to obey today."}, {"p": "*Psalm 119:105* puts it this way:"}, {"v": "Your word is a lamp to my feet, and a light for my path.", "r": "Psalm 119:105"}, {"p": "In the ancient world, a lamp was a small clay bowl filled with olive oil, with a wick resting on its edge. Travelers at night would hold it low, close to their feet, so they wouldn’t trip on rocks or fall into a ditch. The psalmist chose that image on purpose. God’s Word is not a floodlight that removes every mystery from life. It is **a faithful lamp that keeps you from stumbling** — close, steady, and always enough for the step in front of you."}, {"h": "The Light Has a Name"}, {"p": "Centuries later, Jesus stood in the temple courts during the Feast of Tabernacles. Every night of that festival, enormous golden lampstands were lit, and it was said that their glow could be seen across Jerusalem. Into that setting, Jesus made one of the boldest statements anyone has ever made:"}, {"v": "Again, therefore, Jesus spoke to them, saying, “I am the light of the world. He who follows me will not walk in the darkness, but will have the light of life.”", "r": "John 8:12"}, {"p": "Notice what He did not say. He did not say, “I will give you some light,” or “I will show you the way to the light.” He said, **“I am the light.”** Light is not just something Jesus offers; it is who He is. That changes the question we ask. Instead of “God, show me the whole plan,” we begin to ask, “Lord, how do I stay close to You today?”"}, {"p": "And look carefully at the promise: whoever follows Him “will not walk in the darkness.” It doesn’t say darkness will disappear from the world. Hard seasons still come. Questions still keep us awake at night. But we will not be *walking in* the darkness, because the Light walks with us."}, {"h": "Reflecting on the Light: Two Truths to Hold"}, {"p": "When you feel lost or unsure about what comes next, here are two truths worth holding on to:"}, {"p": "1. **God’s guidance is usually step by step.** If you are waiting for the full picture before you obey, you may wait forever. Faith often looks like doing the next right thing — reading, praying, forgiving, showing up — and trusting that the next step will be lit when you need it. God is not withholding the map to frustrate you; He is inviting you to walk *with* Him instead of simply walking *ahead* by yourself."}, {"p": "2. **Darkness never has the final word.** Whatever you are facing — anxiety at night, grief that lingers, confusion about a decision — the darkness is not stronger than the One who calls Himself the Light. John wrote this at the very beginning of his Gospel:"}, {"v": "The light shines in the darkness, and the darkness hasn’t overcome it.", "r": "John 1:5"}, {"p": "The darkness has tried. It has not won. It never will."}, {"h": "“Ner”: The Lamp at Your Feet"}, {"p": "The Hebrew word for “lamp” in Psalm 119 is **ner** (נֵר). It is the same word used for the lamp that burned in the tabernacle, the one the priests were told to keep lit continually before the Lord. A *ner* was not dramatic. It didn’t blaze; it glowed. But it was faithful. It burned through the whole night if someone kept adding oil."}, {"p": "That is a beautiful picture of how God’s Word works in an ordinary life. Most days, reading Scripture won’t feel like fireworks. It will feel like a small, steady glow. But as you keep returning to it — a few verses in the morning, a prayer at night — **the lamp keeps burning**, and you find that you have enough light to take the next step, and the next, and the next."}, {"h": "Your Step Today"}, {"p": "Maybe you opened this app because something in your life feels dark or uncertain. Maybe you simply want to know God more. Either way, here is the good news: you don’t need to fix everything today. You don’t need to understand every chapter of the Bible or have a perfect prayer life. You just need to take one step toward the Light."}, {"p": "What does that look like? It might mean reading today’s verse again before bed. It might mean telling God honestly, “I don’t know what’s next, but I want to follow You.” It might mean making one decision today based on what you already know is right, even if you can’t see where it leads."}, {"p": "**You don’t need the whole map. You need the Light. And the Light is already with you.**"}, {"p": "Great job finishing today’s lesson! Tomorrow we’ll discover something even more personal: the God who lights your path also knows you — and calls you — by name."}], "quiz": [{"q": "In Psalm 119:105, what does God’s Word do for us?", "o": ["It reveals every detail of our future", "It lights the path one step at a time", "It removes every difficulty from our lives", "It replaces the need for prayer"], "a": 1, "e": "The lamp image shows God guiding us step by step — enough light to keep walking, not a full map of the future."}, {"q": "What did Jesus say about himself in John 8:12?", "o": ["“I am the good shepherd”", "“I am the bread of life”", "“I am the light of the world”", "“I am the true vine”"], "a": 2, "e": "Jesus said, “I am the light of the world. He who follows me will not walk in the darkness, but will have the light of life.”"}, {"q": "According to John 8:12, who will not walk in darkness?", "o": ["Those who never make mistakes", "Those who follow Jesus", "Those who understand all of Scripture", "Those who avoid difficult seasons"], "a": 1, "e": "The promise is for those who follow him. It’s about staying close to Jesus, not about being perfect."}, {"q": "What is the main invitation of today’s devotional?", "o": ["Read the whole Bible this week", "Wait until you have all the answers", "Take one step toward the light today", "Solve every problem before praying"], "a": 2, "e": "Faith grows one step at a time. Today’s invitation is to take a single, sincere step toward God."}], "action": "Write Psalm 119:105 on a card or sticky note and put it where you start your day. Each morning this week, read it before you check your phone.", "journal": "What is one area of your life where you wish you could see the whole map? What would it look like to trust God for just the next step?", "closing": {"t": "Again, therefore, Jesus spoke to them, saying, “I am the light of the world. He who follows me will not walk in the darkness, but will have the light of life.”", "r": "John 8:12"}}, "1-1-2": {"image": "name", "prayer": ["Lord,", "It amazes me that you know my name. Not just my name — my story, my fears, the things I’ve never said out loud.", "Some days I feel invisible, or like I’m just one face in a crowd. Remind me today that I am known and wanted by you.", "Help me to recognize your voice when you call, and give me the courage to answer.", "In Jesus’ name, Amen."], "devotional": [{"h": "Known in a Crowd"}, {"p": "Think about the last time someone remembered your name when you didn’t expect it. Maybe it was a teacher you hadn’t seen in years, a barista who greeted you before you ordered, or a colleague from another department who said, “Hi, Sarah!” as you passed. Something small happens inside you in that moment. **I was noticed. I matter here.**"}, {"p": "Now think about the opposite feeling — standing in a crowded room where no one knows you, or sending a message that never gets an answer, or wondering if anyone would notice if you simply stopped showing up. Many people carry that feeling quietly for years. They are surrounded by people, yet they feel unseen."}, {"p": "Into that feeling, Scripture speaks a message that is almost too good to believe: **the God who made the stars knows your name** — not as data, not as a number, but personally, warmly, the way a parent knows the name of a child."}, {"p": "God spoke these words through the prophet Isaiah to a people who felt forgotten. They were far from home, surrounded by a culture that didn’t share their faith, wondering if God had moved on without them. His answer was not a lecture. It was a name. *Isaiah 43:1* tells us:"}, {"v": "But now Yahweh who created you, Jacob, and he who formed you, Israel, says: “Don’t be afraid, for I have redeemed you. I have called you by your name. You are mine.", "r": "Isaiah 43:1"}, {"p": "Look at the order of that verse. First, God reminds them who He is: the One who **created** and **formed** them. Then He tells them what He has done: He has **redeemed** them. And finally, He says what they are: **“You are mine.”** Their identity didn’t come from their circumstances, their failures, or what others said about them. It came from the One who called them by name."}, {"h": "The Shepherd Who Calls"}, {"p": "Jesus took this truth and wrapped it in a picture everyone in His day would understand. In ancient Israel, several families often kept their flocks together in one stone sheepfold overnight. In the morning, each shepherd would come to the gate and call out. The sheep would lift their heads — and only his sheep would come, because they recognized his voice."}, {"v": "The gatekeeper opens the gate for him, and the sheep listen to his voice. He calls his own sheep by name, and leads them out.", "r": "John 10:3"}, {"p": "Some shepherds even had a name for each sheep, often based on a marking or a personality trait. That is the picture Jesus chose to describe His relationship with you. **You are not lost in the flock.** He knows the details — your history, your fears, the things you’ve never said out loud — and He still calls you by name."}, {"h": "Reflecting on Being Known: Two Truths to Remember"}, {"p": "1. **You are known completely — and loved completely.** Many of us fear that if someone truly knew us, they would walk away. With God, the opposite is true. He already knows everything, and He stays. David marveled at this in Psalm 139: God knows when he sits and when he rises, his thoughts from far away, every word before it’s spoken. Being fully known by God is not a threat; it is the safest place in the world."}, {"v": "Yahweh, you have searched me, and you know me.", "r": "Psalm 139:1"}, {"p": "2. **Your identity is given, not earned.** In a world that constantly asks “What do you do?” and “What have you achieved?”, God speaks a different word over you: *“You are mine.”* Your worth is not the sum of your performance, your appearance, or your popularity. It rests on the fact that you belong to Him."}, {"h": "“Qara”: To Call by Name"}, {"p": "The Hebrew word translated “called” in Isaiah 43:1 is **qara** (קָרָא). It means to call out, to summon, to proclaim — but when it’s used with someone’s name, it carries the idea of claiming and appointing them. In the ancient world, naming something was an act of relationship and responsibility. When God says, “I have called you by your name,” He is saying, **“I have claimed you. You are my responsibility. I will take care of you.”**"}, {"p": "That is why the very next verse continues with a promise: when you pass through the waters, He will be with you; when you walk through the fire, you will not be burned. The God who calls you by name doesn’t call you and then leave you. He walks with you."}, {"h": "Learning to Recognize His Voice"}, {"p": "Recognizing a voice takes time. You learned to recognize your mother’s voice, your best friend’s laugh, your spouse’s footsteps in the hallway — not through one encounter, but through many. The same is true with God. Every time you open His Word, pray honestly, or pause to reflect, **you become more familiar with how He speaks.**"}, {"v": "My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me.", "r": "John 10:27"}, {"p": "Notice the three movements in that verse: His sheep **hear**, He **knows**, they **follow**. It’s a relationship, not a formula. You don’t have to be a spiritual expert to hear Him. You just have to keep showing up, keep listening, and keep taking the next step."}, {"p": "So today, let this settle deep into your heart: **you are not invisible. You are not forgotten. You are known, called, and claimed by God.**"}, {"p": "Great job finishing today’s lesson! Tomorrow we’ll look at one of the most freeing truths in the Bible — that God’s love came to you before you ever earned it."}], "quiz": [{"q": "In Isaiah 43:1, what does God say to his people?", "o": ["“Prove yourselves worthy of me”", "“I have called you by your name. You are mine.”", "“Find your own way back to me”", "“I will call you when you are ready”"], "a": 1, "e": "God speaks personally: he has redeemed them, called them by name, and claims them as his own."}, {"q": "Why did the sheep in John 10 follow their shepherd?", "o": ["They were forced through the gate", "They followed the biggest group", "They knew his voice", "They were the hungriest"], "a": 2, "e": "The sheep recognized the voice of their own shepherd — a picture of a close, personal relationship."}, {"q": "How do we learn to recognize God’s voice, according to the devotional?", "o": ["By waiting for a dramatic sign", "By spending time with him in his Word and prayer", "By never asking questions", "By comparing ourselves to others"], "a": 1, "e": "Just like with people we love, familiarity grows through time spent together — in Scripture, prayer, and reflection."}, {"q": "What is the central truth of today’s lesson?", "o": ["God is distant and hard to reach", "We must earn God’s attention", "You are known, called, and belong to God", "Only a few people hear God’s voice"], "a": 2, "e": "God knows you personally and calls you his own — not because of performance, but because of his love."}], "action": "Write your first name at the top of a page, and under it write Isaiah 43:1. Read it out loud once today, replacing “Jacob” and “Israel” with your own name.", "journal": "When have you felt unseen or forgotten? How does it change things to know God calls you by name?", "closing": {"t": "My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me.", "r": "John 10:27"}}, "1-1-3": {"image": "gift", "prayer": ["Father,", "I confess that I often try to earn what you have already given. I measure my worth by how well I perform, and I feel far from you when I fall short.", "Thank you that your love came to me before I ever deserved it. Thank you for the cross, where Jesus paid what I could never pay.", "Teach me to rest in your grace — not as an excuse to stay the same, but as the soil where real change can grow.", "In Jesus’ name, Amen."], "devotional": [{"h": "The Bill You Keep Trying to Pay"}, {"p": "Imagine a friend invites you to dinner. When the check arrives, she slides it toward herself and says, “This one’s on me.” A week later, you try to pay her back. She laughs and says, “It was a gift.” A month later, you try again. And again. Eventually, something becomes a little sad: you simply can’t believe someone would be generous with you **without expecting something in return.**"}, {"p": "Many sincere believers live exactly that way with God. In our heads, we know salvation is a gift. But in our hearts, we keep trying to pay the bill. One more good deed. One more perfect week. One more promise to do better. And when we fail — because we always do — we feel far from God, as if His love had been withdrawn because we missed a payment."}, {"p": "The Bible tells a very different story. *Romans 5:8* says:"}, {"v": "But God commends his own love toward us, in that while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us.", "r": "Romans 5:8"}, {"p": "Read that slowly. God did not wait for us to clean ourselves up. He didn’t wait for us to become religious enough, disciplined enough, or good enough. **He showed His love while we were still sinners** — while we were still running in the wrong direction. His love is not a reward for our progress. It is the starting point of it."}, {"h": "Grace, Then Good Works"}, {"p": "The apostle Paul spent much of his life as a man who tried to earn God’s approval. He was a Pharisee, meticulous about the law, proud of his record. When he met Jesus, everything turned upside down. Years later, he explained the order of things with remarkable clarity:"}, {"v": "for by grace you have been saved through faith, and that not of yourselves; it is the gift of God, not of works, that no one would boast.", "r": "Ephesians 2:8-9"}, {"p": "We are saved **by grace, through faith**. Not by effort. Not by a spiritual résumé. Not by being better than the people around us. And Paul draws the obvious conclusion: no one gets to boast. But there is a second conclusion hiding in that verse, one that is just as important — **no one has to despair.** If salvation depended on your performance, you could never be sure you had done enough. Because it depends on God’s grace, you can rest."}, {"p": "But Paul doesn’t stop there. In the very next sentence, he explains what grace produces:"}, {"v": "For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works, which God prepared before that we would walk in them.", "r": "Ephesians 2:10"}, {"p": "Good works are not the price of God’s love; they are the fruit of it. **A tree doesn’t grow fruit in order to become a tree. It grows fruit because it is one.** When you know you are loved, obedience stops being a heavy obligation and becomes a grateful response."}, {"h": "Reflecting on Grace: Two Truths That Set You Free"}, {"p": "1. **God’s love is not a wage.** A wage is something you earn; a gift is something you receive. If you treat God’s love like a paycheck, you will always feel underpaid or overdue. But if you receive it as a gift, you can finally stop measuring yourself every day. Your worst day doesn’t cancel His love, and your best day doesn’t increase it. It was never about the scorecard."}, {"p": "2. **Grace is not permission to stay the same.** Some people fear that if they stop striving, they’ll stop growing. But grace is not a couch; it’s soil. It is the environment where real change finally becomes possible. People who know they are loved don’t stop doing good — they start doing it for the right reasons."}, {"h": "“Charis” and “Poiēma”: Gift and Masterpiece"}, {"p": "The Greek word for grace in Ephesians 2 is **charis** (χάρις). In everyday Greek, it described a favor freely given, a kindness that the giver chose to offer without obligation. It is closely related to *chara*, the word for joy. Grace and joy come from the same family — which makes sense. **Receiving a gift you could never earn naturally leads to joy.**"}, {"p": "Then, in verse 10, Paul calls us God’s “workmanship.” The Greek word is **poiēma** (ποίημα) — the root of our English word *poem*. It describes something crafted with care and intention, a work of art. You are not God’s project to fix. **You are His poem, still being written.** The good works He prepared for you are not tests to pass; they are lines He wants to write through your life."}, {"h": "Resting in What Is Finished"}, {"p": "Why do we keep trying so hard, as if we needed to win God’s affection? Perhaps because we live in a world where almost everything is earned — grades, salaries, approval, even love from some people. Unconditional love feels strange to us. We keep waiting for the catch."}, {"p": "But the cross is God’s way of saying there is no catch. Jesus paid what you could never pay. When He said, “It is finished,” He meant it. **You can stop trying to settle a bill that has already been paid in full.**"}, {"p": "Today, take a deep breath and receive what God is offering. Thank Him for a gift you could never afford. Then let that gratitude spill over into kindness toward someone else — not to earn anything, but because you have been loved so well."}, {"p": "**Grace first. Then everything else.**"}, {"p": "Great job finishing today’s lesson! Tomorrow we’ll explore what it looks like to come to God honestly in prayer — without pretending, without performing."}], "quiz": [{"q": "According to Romans 5:8, when did God show his love for us?", "o": ["After we started obeying him", "While we were still sinners", "Once we understood the Bible", "When we promised to do better"], "a": 1, "e": "God demonstrated his love through Christ’s death while we were still sinners — before any improvement on our part."}, {"q": "Ephesians 2:8-9 says we are saved by…", "o": ["Grace through faith", "Good works and effort", "Religious tradition", "Our family heritage"], "a": 0, "e": "Salvation is the gift of God, received by grace through faith — not of works, so no one can boast."}, {"q": "What does Ephesians 2:10 say about good works?", "o": ["They are how we earn salvation", "They don’t matter at all", "God prepared them for us to walk in", "Only leaders are called to them"], "a": 2, "e": "We are God’s workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works that he prepared beforehand."}, {"q": "Which picture did the devotional use for good works?", "o": ["A ladder we climb to God", "Fruit that grows from a living tree", "A scale balancing good and bad", "A debt we slowly pay off"], "a": 1, "e": "A tree doesn’t grow fruit to become a tree. Good works are the fruit of grace, not the price of it."}], "action": "Think of one thing you’ve been doing to ‘earn’ God’s approval. Today, do one kind act for someone else — not to earn anything, but simply because you’ve been loved.", "journal": "Where do you feel the pressure to earn God’s love? What would change if you truly believed it is a gift?", "closing": {"t": "But God commends his own love toward us, in that while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us.", "r": "Romans 5:8"}}, "1-1-4": {"image": "prayer", "prayer": ["Lord,", "Sometimes I don’t know what to say to you. Sometimes my prayers sound polished, but my heart is somewhere else.", "Today I want to be honest. Here are my worries, my disappointments, and my hopes — all of them.", "Thank you that I don’t need perfect words to come to you. Meet me in the honest places.", "In Jesus’ name, Amen."], "devotional": [{"h": "Prayer Is Not a Performance"}, {"p": "A little girl climbs onto her father’s lap at the end of the day and starts talking. She tells him about a scraped knee, a friend who was mean at recess, a drawing she’s proud of, and a strange dream she had the night before. She doesn’t organize her thoughts into neat points. She doesn’t use impressive words. She just pours it all out — **and her father loves every second of it.**"}, {"p": "Somewhere along the way, many of us learned that prayer has to sound a certain way. We heard others pray with beautiful phrases and long sentences, and our own prayers felt too simple, too messy, or too small. So we either started performing — saying what we thought God wanted to hear — or we stopped praying altogether, except in emergencies."}, {"p": "But Scripture invites something far simpler and far more real:"}, {"v": "Trust in him at all times, you people. Pour out your heart before him. God is a refuge for us.", "r": "Psalm 62:8"}, {"p": "**Pour out your heart.** Not a summary. Not a cleaned-up version. The Psalms are full of raw, honest prayers — joy and grief, fear and anger, confusion and gratitude, sometimes all in the same chapter. God included them in the Bible to show us that **He is not shocked by your feelings. He is a refuge for them.**"}, {"h": "“Lord, Teach Us to Pray”"}, {"p": "If prayer came naturally to everyone, the disciples would never have asked for help. But they did. After watching Jesus pray, one of them said, *“Lord, teach us to pray”* (Luke 11:1). Notice: they didn’t ask Him to teach them to preach or to perform miracles. They asked to learn how to talk with the Father. And Jesus answered:"}, {"v": "He said to them, “When you pray, say, ‘Our Father in heaven, may your name be kept holy. May your Kingdom come. May your will be done on earth, as it is in heaven. Give us day by day our daily bread. Forgive us our sins, for we ourselves also forgive everyone who is indebted to us. Bring us not into temptation, but deliver us from the evil one.’ ”", "r": "Luke 11:2-4"}, {"p": "In just a few lines, Jesus shows us the whole shape of a healthy prayer life. It begins with **“Father”** — intimacy, not distance. *“May your name be kept holy”* — reverence for who He is. *“May your Kingdom come”* — surrendering our plans to His. *“Give us day by day our daily bread”* — honest dependence for today’s needs. *“Forgive us our sins”* — mercy received and passed on. *“Bring us not into temptation”* — asking for His protection."}, {"p": "It’s short. It’s simple. And it covers everything that matters. **Jesus never measured prayer by its length, only by its heart.**"}, {"h": "Thank, Tell, Ask, Trust"}, {"p": "Drawing from the prayer Jesus taught and the prayers of the Psalms, here is an easy way to remember a balanced conversation with God — four simple words:"}, {"p": "**T — Thank**"}, {"p": "Start with gratitude. Before you bring a single request, name something God has already done — a meal, a friend, a breath, a second chance. Gratitude reminds your heart who you’re talking to. Paul writes:"}, {"v": "Always rejoice. Pray without ceasing. In everything give thanks, for this is the will of God in Christ Jesus toward you.", "r": "1 Thessalonians 5:16-18"}, {"p": "**T — Tell**"}, {"p": "Tell God what is really going on inside you — honestly, without polishing it. David didn’t hide his troubles; he named them:"}, {"v": "I pour out my complaint before him. I tell him my troubles.", "r": "Psalm 142:2"}, {"p": "Confession belongs here too. When you tell God where you’ve fallen short, you’re not informing Him of anything new — you’re opening the door for His mercy to come in."}, {"p": "**A — Ask**"}, {"p": "Ask for what you need, big or small. Jesus didn’t say “only ask for spiritual things.” He said:"}, {"v": "“Ask, and it will be given you. Seek, and you will find. Knock, and it will be opened for you.", "r": "Matthew 7:7"}, {"p": "Your job interview, your child’s fever, your tight budget, your loneliness on a Friday night — **nothing is too small or too ordinary to bring to God.**"}, {"p": "**T — Trust**"}, {"p": "Finally, place the outcome in His hands. This is where prayer becomes rest instead of another way to worry:"}, {"v": "In nothing be anxious, but in everything, by prayer and petition with thanksgiving, let your requests be made known to God. And the peace of God, which surpasses all understanding, will guard your hearts and your thoughts in Christ Jesus.", "r": "Philippians 4:6-7"}, {"p": "The situation may not change immediately. The test results may still be pending. But something inside you changes. **Prayer moves the weight from your shoulders into God’s hands.**"}, {"h": "“Merimnaō” and “Phroureō”: Divided Minds and Guarded Hearts"}, {"p": "The Greek word Paul uses for “anxious” is **merimnaō** (μεριμνάω). Many teachers connect it to the idea of being pulled in different directions — a mind divided, torn between a hundred futures that haven’t happened yet. That is exactly what worry feels like."}, {"p": "Then Paul says God’s peace will **guard** your heart. The word is **phroureō** (φρουρέω), a military term for soldiers standing watch over a city. Paul was writing to Philippi, a Roman colony where soldiers were a familiar sight. The picture is powerful: **when you pray, God stations His peace like soldiers at the gates of your heart.**"}, {"h": "No Perfect Words Required"}, {"p": "The beauty of prayer is that, even though it has a shape — thank, tell, ask, trust — the heart of it is not a formula. There are no perfect words you have to say, no special time you have to wait for, no performance to pass. **Prayer is not a ceremony — it’s a conversation with your Father.**"}, {"p": "Jesus once told a story about two men praying in the temple. One prayed a long, polished prayer about how good he was. The other could barely lift his eyes and simply said, “God, be merciful to me, a sinner.” Jesus said it was the second man who went home right with God. Whether your words are eloquent or broken, whispered or cried out, God hears. And He has promised to answer:"}, {"v": "‘Call to me, and I will answer you, and will show you great and difficult things, which you don’t know.’", "r": "Jeremiah 33:3"}, {"p": "So remember the little girl on her father’s lap. She didn’t need the right words — she just needed to come. **You don’t need perfect words either. You need an honest heart and a Father who listens — and you already have Him.**"}, {"p": "Great job finishing today’s lesson! Tomorrow we’ll talk about one of the hardest parts of faith: what to do when you’re waiting and God seems quiet."}], "quiz": [{"q": "What does Psalm 62:8 invite us to do?", "o": ["Hide our feelings from God", "Pour out our hearts before him", "Pray only when things are good", "Use formal words in prayer"], "a": 1, "e": "The psalmist calls us to trust God at all times and pour out our hearts before him, because God is our refuge."}, {"q": "What did the disciples ask Jesus in Luke 11:1?", "o": ["“Lord, teach us to preach”", "“Lord, show us a miracle”", "“Lord, teach us to pray”", "“Lord, make us wise”"], "a": 2, "e": "After watching Jesus pray, a disciple asked, “Lord, teach us to pray” — and Jesus answered with the prayer in Luke 11:2-4."}, {"q": "What do the four words of today’s prayer pattern stand for?", "o": ["Think, Teach, Act, Try", "Thank, Tell, Ask, Trust", "Thank, Testify, Ask, Talk", "Talk, Tell, Accept, Try"], "a": 1, "e": "Thank God for what he has done, tell him honestly what’s inside you, ask for what you need, and trust him with the outcome."}, {"q": "What is the main message of “No Perfect Words Required”?", "o": ["Prayer needs the right words and setting", "Prayer is about rituals and formality", "Prayer is an honest conversation with God, not a performance", "Prayer only works at specific times of day"], "a": 2, "e": "Prayer is not a ceremony — it’s a conversation with your Father. Whether your words are eloquent or broken, God hears."}], "action": "Write down three names — people you love or people who are hurting. Pray for each one by name, out loud, using Thank, Tell, Ask, Trust.", "journal": "What is something you’ve been carrying but haven’t honestly brought to God? Write it here as a prayer.", "closing": {"t": "And the peace of God, which surpasses all understanding, will guard your hearts and your thoughts in Christ Jesus.", "r": "Philippians 4:7"}}, "1-1-5": {"image": "wait", "prayer": ["Father,", "Waiting is hard for me. I want answers now, solutions now, open doors now.", "Help me believe that you are working even when I can’t see it. Renew my strength in the in-between seasons.", "Make me patient, not passive — trusting you while I stay faithful in today.", "In Jesus’ name, Amen."], "devotional": [{"h": "What Happens Underground"}, {"p": "There’s an old gardener’s story about a kind of bamboo that, after planting, shows almost nothing above the soil for years. The gardener waters it faithfully, season after season, and sees little more than a small shoot. It would be easy to give up and assume nothing was happening. But underground, something remarkable is taking place: **a massive, interwoven root system is forming.** Then, when the time is right, the shoots rise astonishingly fast — growing many feet in a matter of weeks."}, {"p": "Waiting seasons can feel like wasted time. You pray and nothing seems to move. You apply and the door stays closed. You hope for healing, for reconciliation, for a breakthrough, and the calendar keeps turning. In those seasons, it’s tempting to conclude that God has forgotten you, or that you must be doing something wrong."}, {"p": "But Scripture tells us that **God often does His deepest work underground** — in our character, our trust, and our roots — long before anything shows on the surface. *Psalm 27:14* speaks directly to the waiting heart:"}, {"v": "Wait for Yahweh. Be strong, and let your heart take courage. Yes, wait for Yahweh.", "r": "Psalm 27:14"}, {"p": "Notice that David doesn’t say, “Wait, and try not to feel anything.” He says, “Be strong, and let your heart take courage.” Waiting on God is not passive. **It takes strength to keep trusting when you can’t see results.** It takes courage to hope again after disappointment."}, {"h": "Strength for the Long Road"}, {"p": "Isaiah wrote to people who had waited a very long time — generations, in fact — for God’s promises to be fulfilled. They were tired. Some had stopped believing anything would ever change. Into that weariness, God spoke one of the most beloved promises in the Bible:"}, {"v": "but those who wait for Yahweh will renew their strength. They will mount up with wings like eagles. They will run, and not be weary. They will walk, and not faint.", "r": "Isaiah 40:31"}, {"p": "Look closely at the order: **soar, run, walk.** We tend to read it as if the greatest miracle is to mount up with wings like eagles. But in a long season of waiting, sometimes the greatest miracle is the last one — that you keep walking and don’t faint. On some days God gives you wings. On others, He gives you just enough strength to put one foot in front of the other. **Both are His gift.**"}, {"h": "Reflecting on Waiting: Two Truths for the In-Between"}, {"p": "1. **Waiting is not wasted.** Joseph waited years in a prison before he stood in Pharaoh’s palace. Moses spent forty years in the wilderness before he led Israel out of Egypt. David was anointed king as a teenager but spent years running from Saul before he took the throne. In each case, the waiting was not an interruption of God’s plan — **it was part of it.** God was forming the person who could carry the promise."}, {"p": "2. **God is good to those who wait for Him.** That doesn’t mean everything turns out the way we imagined. It means that God Himself is good, and that He is especially near to those who keep seeking Him in the in-between. Jeremiah wrote these words while sitting in the ruins of Jerusalem:"}, {"v": "Yahweh is good to those who wait for him, to the soul who seeks him.", "r": "Lamentations 3:25"}, {"h": "“Qavah”: Waiting With Expectation"}, {"p": "The Hebrew word translated “wait” in both Psalm 27 and Isaiah 40 is **qavah** (קָוָה). It doesn’t describe passively sitting around. It carries the sense of **eagerly looking for, expecting, hoping** — like a watchman on the city wall scanning the horizon for the first light of dawn. It belongs to the same family of words as *tikvah*, the Hebrew word for hope."}, {"p": "Many teachers also point out that the root of *qavah* is connected to the idea of a cord or line — strands twisted together until they become strong. It’s a beautiful picture: **as you wait on God, your life is being bound more tightly to His.** What feels like weakness is actually becoming strength."}, {"h": "Faithful in Today"}, {"p": "So what do you do while you wait? You stay faithful in the assignments God has already given you. Love the people in front of you. Do your work with excellence. Keep showing up in prayer, even when your prayers feel repetitive. Take care of your body and your soul. **Waiting on God doesn’t mean putting your life on hold — it means living today with your hope anchored in Him.**"}, {"p": "And remember the gardener’s bamboo. Just because you can’t see growth doesn’t mean nothing is happening. The roots you grow in this season will hold you steady when the season changes."}, {"p": "**Your waiting is not empty. God is working underground.**"}, {"p": "Great job finishing today’s lesson! Tomorrow we’ll face one of the most common struggles of the human heart — fear — and discover the command God repeats more than almost any other."}], "quiz": [{"q": "What does Psalm 27:14 encourage us to do?", "o": ["Give up when God is silent", "Wait for the Lord with courage", "Find another way on our own", "Demand an immediate answer"], "a": 1, "e": "“Wait for Yahweh. Be strong, and let your heart take courage.” Waiting is paired with strength and courage."}, {"q": "In Scripture, waiting on God is best described as…", "o": ["Doing nothing", "Expectant, active trust", "Losing hope slowly", "A punishment"], "a": 1, "e": "Biblical waiting is like a watchman expecting the dawn — active hope, not passive resignation."}, {"q": "What does Isaiah 40:31 promise to those who wait on the Lord?", "o": ["Instant answers", "Renewed strength", "No more problems", "Riches and fame"], "a": 1, "e": "Those who wait for Yahweh will renew their strength — they will soar, run, and walk without fainting."}, {"q": "What image did the devotional use for waiting seasons?", "o": ["A locked door", "Roots growing underground", "A broken clock", "An empty field forever"], "a": 1, "e": "Like bamboo forming roots before it rises, God often does deep work in us when nothing seems to be happening."}], "action": "Name one thing you are waiting on. Today, write down one faithful step you can take while you wait — and do it.", "journal": "What are you waiting on right now? What might God be growing in you underground during this season?", "closing": {"t": "but those who wait for Yahweh will renew their strength. They will mount up with wings like eagles. They will run, and not be weary. They will walk, and not faint.", "r": "Isaiah 40:31"}}, "1-1-6": {"image": "shield", "prayer": ["Lord,", "You know the fears I carry — some loud, some quiet. Fear of the future, of failing, of losing what I love.", "Today I choose to listen to your voice above the voice of fear. You are with me. You are my God.", "Strengthen me, help me, and hold me up with your righteous hand.", "In Jesus’ name, Amen."], "devotional": [{"h": "The Most Repeated Command"}, {"p": "If you read through the Bible looking for God’s most frequent instruction, you might expect something like “try harder” or “be better.” But one of the phrases that appears again and again is much gentler: **“Don’t be afraid.”** God says it to Abraham in the night, to Joshua at the edge of the Promised Land, to Gideon hiding in a winepress, to Mary in Nazareth, to the shepherds in the fields, and to the disciples in a storm."}, {"p": "Why does God repeat it so often? Because He knows how easily fear takes the steering wheel of our lives. Fear tells us to stay small, to avoid risks, to protect ourselves at all costs. Fear can make us cling to control, avoid difficult conversations, and say no to the very things God is inviting us into."}, {"p": "Through the prophet Isaiah, God spoke to a people surrounded by threats on every side. *Isaiah 41:10* says:"}, {"v": "Don’t you be afraid, for I am with you. Don’t be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you. Yes, I will help you. Yes, I will uphold you with the right hand of my righteousness.", "r": "Isaiah 41:10"}, {"p": "Look carefully at the reasons God gives. He doesn’t say, “Don’t be afraid, because nothing bad will happen.” He says, **“Don’t be afraid, for I am with you. I am your God.”** The cure for fear in the Bible is not the absence of danger. **It is the presence of God.**"}, {"p": "And then God makes three promises, one after another: *I will strengthen you. I will help you. I will uphold you.* Strength for what is ahead, help in what is now, and a hand that holds you when you feel like falling."}, {"h": "Courage for the Next Assignment"}, {"p": "When Moses died, Joshua inherited one of the most overwhelming jobs in history: lead an entire nation across a flooded river into a land full of fortified cities. Joshua had seen giants in that land with his own eyes. If anyone had a reason to be afraid, it was him."}, {"p": "God didn’t give Joshua a strategy manual first. He gave him a promise of presence:"}, {"v": "Haven’t I commanded you? Be strong and courageous. Don’t be afraid. Don’t be dismayed, for Yahweh your God is with you wherever you go.”", "r": "Joshua 1:9"}, {"p": "Notice that God commands courage — “Be strong and courageous” — but He doesn’t command Joshua to *feel* fearless. **Biblical courage is not the absence of fear; it is moving forward in obedience while the fear is still there**, because you trust the One who walks beside you."}, {"h": "Reflecting on Fear: Two Things to Remember"}, {"p": "1. **Fear is a feeling, not a fact.** Fear often predicts disasters that never happen. It speaks in “what ifs” and worst-case scenarios. Feelings are real, and God cares about them — but they are not always reliable guides. When fear speaks, you can answer it with what is true: God is with you, God is for you, and God is able."}, {"p": "2. **Courage grows by taking small steps.** You don’t have to conquer every fear today. Joshua crossed the Jordan one step at a time, and the priests had to put their feet in the water before it parted. Sometimes God waits for our first step of trust before He shows us His power."}, {"h": "“Deilia”: The Spirit We Were Not Given"}, {"p": "Near the end of his life, Paul wrote to his young friend Timothy, who seems to have struggled with timidity. Paul reminded him of something essential:"}, {"v": "For God didn’t give us a spirit of fear, but of power, love, and self-control.", "r": "2 Timothy 1:7"}, {"p": "The Greek word translated “fear” here is **deilia** (δειλία). It refers to timidity or cowardice — the kind of fear that makes us shrink back from what God has called us to do. Paul’s point is clear: **that kind of fear doesn’t come from God.** Instead, the Holy Spirit gives us three things: **power** to face what’s in front of us, **love** that pushes us outward toward others, and **self-control** — a sound, steady mind that isn’t ruled by panic."}, {"h": "Naming Your Fear"}, {"p": "Fear grows in the dark and shrinks in the light. One of the most practical things you can do today is to name the fear that has been loudest in your mind. Is it fear of failure? Of being alone? Of not having enough? Of losing someone you love? Of what people think?"}, {"p": "Once you name it, hold it next to God’s promise. **Fear says, “You’re on your own.” God says, “I am with you.”** Fear says, “You won’t make it.” God says, “I will strengthen you.” Fear says, “It’s all going to fall apart.” God says, “I will uphold you with my righteous right hand.”"}, {"p": "You get to choose which voice you will follow today."}, {"p": "**You may still feel afraid. But you are not alone — and that changes everything.**"}, {"p": "Great job finishing today’s lesson! Tomorrow is our chapter review. We’ll look back at everything God has planted in your heart this week and see how deep your roots have grown."}], "quiz": [{"q": "In Isaiah 41:10, what reason does God give for not fearing?", "o": ["Nothing bad will happen", "“I am with you… I am your God”", "You are strong enough alone", "Fear is not real"], "a": 1, "e": "God’s answer to fear is his presence: “Don’t you be afraid, for I am with you. Don’t be dismayed, for I am your God.”"}, {"q": "What did God tell Joshua in Joshua 1:9?", "o": ["Wait until you feel ready", "Be strong and courageous, for I am with you wherever you go", "Let someone else lead", "Only go if there is no danger"], "a": 1, "e": "God commanded Joshua to be strong and courageous, because Yahweh his God would be with him wherever he went."}, {"q": "How did the devotional define biblical courage?", "o": ["Never feeling afraid", "Moving forward in obedience while fear is still there", "Taking unnecessary risks", "Hiding your feelings"], "a": 1, "e": "Courage is trusting God enough to obey even when fear is present."}, {"q": "What does 2 Timothy 1:7 say God has given us?", "o": ["A spirit of fear", "A spirit of power, love, and self-control", "A life without trials", "Perfect knowledge of the future"], "a": 1, "e": "God didn’t give us a spirit of fear, but of power, love, and self-control."}], "action": "Write down your loudest fear. Next to it, write Isaiah 41:10. Each time the fear shows up today, read the verse out loud.", "journal": "What fear has been steering your decisions lately? What would one courageous step look like this week?", "closing": {"t": "Haven’t I commanded you? Be strong and courageous. Don’t be afraid. Don’t be dismayed, for Yahweh your God is with you wherever you go.”", "r": "Joshua 1:9"}}, "1-1-7": {"image": "tree", "prayer": ["Father,", "Thank you for this first week with you. Thank you for light for my path, for calling me by name, for grace I didn’t earn, for listening to my honest prayers, and for being with me in waiting and in fear.", "Let these truths go deep. Make my roots strong so I can stand when the winds come.", "I want to keep walking with you, one day at a time.", "In Jesus’ name, Amen."], "devotional": [{"h": "A Tree by the Water"}, {"p": "You’ve made it through your first chapter. That is worth celebrating. Building a habit of meeting with God every day is not easy — life is busy, distractions are everywhere, and some days you probably didn’t feel like opening this app. But you kept showing up. **That faithfulness matters more than you know.**"}, {"p": "Before we move forward, let’s pause and look at what has been planted. The prophet Jeremiah gave us one of the most beautiful pictures of a faith that lasts. *Jeremiah 17:7-8* says:"}, {"v": "“Blessed is the man who trusts in Yahweh, and whose confidence is in Yahweh. For he will be as a tree planted by the waters, who spreads out its roots by the river, and will not fear when heat comes, but its leaf will be green, and will not be concerned in the year of drought. It won’t cease from yielding fruit.", "r": "Jeremiah 17:7-8"}, {"p": "This tree doesn’t live in a world without heat. Drought still comes. The seasons still change. But its leaves stay green and it keeps bearing fruit because **its roots reach the water.** That is the picture of the faith we’re building together — not a faith that never faces hard seasons, but one that stays alive in them."}, {"p": "Jeremiah contrasts this tree with a shrub in the desert — a person who trusts only in human strength. That shrub has shallow roots and withers when the heat comes. The difference between the two is not the weather. **It’s where the roots go.**"}, {"h": "What Has Been Planted This Week"}, {"p": "Let’s look back at the six roots God has been growing in you:"}, {"p": "1. **Light for the next step.** You don’t need the whole map. God’s Word is a lamp for your feet, and Jesus Himself is the Light of the world."}, {"p": "2. **Called by name.** You are not invisible or forgotten. God knows you completely, calls you personally, and says, “You are mine.”"}, {"p": "3. **Grace first.** God’s love is the root, not the reward. He loved you while you were still a sinner, and good works grow as fruit from that love."}, {"p": "4. **Honest prayer.** You can pour out your heart to God without pretending. Thank, tell, ask, trust — and let His peace stand guard over your mind."}, {"p": "5. **Active waiting.** Waiting is not wasted. God works underground, and those who hope in Him find renewed strength."}, {"p": "6. **Courage with presence.** Fear may still come, but you are not alone. God strengthens you, helps you, and holds you up."}, {"p": "Each of these is a root. Alone, one root might not hold much. **Together, they anchor a life.**"}, {"h": "“Rhizoō”: Rooted and Built Up"}, {"p": "Paul wrote to the believers in Colossae, a church surrounded by competing ideas and philosophies. He wanted them to stay steady, so he gave them this encouragement:"}, {"v": "As therefore you received Christ Jesus, the Lord, walk in him, rooted and built up in him, and established in the faith, even as you were taught, abounding in it in thanksgiving.", "r": "Colossians 2:6-7"}, {"p": "The Greek word for “rooted” is **rhizoō** (ῥιζόω) — from *rhiza*, “root.” Paul uses a form of the word that describes something that has already happened and continues to have effects: you **have been rooted**, and you remain rooted. Your roots in Christ are not something you have to create from scratch every morning. God planted them. Your part is to keep drawing from the source."}, {"p": "Notice, too, how Paul mixes his images. He goes from a tree (*rooted*) to a building (*built up*) to a foundation (*established*). Faith is both organic and structural — **it grows like a tree and stands like a house.** And the sign that it’s healthy? “Abounding in thanksgiving.” A rooted heart is a grateful heart."}, {"h": "Keep Walking in Him"}, {"p": "Paul’s words are a perfect summary of this first chapter: **as you received Christ, so walk in Him.** You didn’t receive Him by being perfect, and you won’t walk with Him by being perfect either. You received Him by faith, and you’ll walk with Him the same way — one day at a time, one step at a time, one honest prayer at a time."}, {"p": "Take a moment today to thank God for what He has done this week. Maybe a verse stood out to you. Maybe a prayer felt different. Maybe you noticed a little more peace than before. Don’t rush past it. **Gratitude waters the roots.**"}, {"p": "**You are a tree planted by the water. Keep drinking from the source, and you will stay green in every season.**"}, {"p": "Congratulations on completing Chapter 1! Tomorrow a new chapter begins: *Walking With Jesus* — where we’ll follow Him through the pages of the Gospels and discover what it means to walk closely with Him. Come back ready, and bring a thankful heart."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Why does the tree in Jeremiah 17:8 stay green during drought?", "o": ["Drought never reaches it", "Its roots reach the water", "It is protected by a wall", "It stops growing in hard seasons"], "a": 1, "e": "The tree is planted by the waters and spreads its roots by the river, so it doesn’t fear when heat comes."}, {"q": "Which truth from this chapter says God’s love is the root, not the reward?", "o": ["Light for the next step", "Grace first", "Active waiting", "Courage with presence"], "a": 1, "e": "Day 3 taught that we are loved before we earn anything — grace is the starting point of change."}, {"q": "According to Colossians 2:6-7, how should we live after receiving Christ?", "o": ["Rooted and built up in him, abounding in thanksgiving", "Waiting for a perfect moment", "Relying on our own strength", "Keeping faith private and quiet"], "a": 0, "e": "Paul says to walk in him, rooted and built up in him, established in the faith, abounding in thanksgiving."}, {"q": "What does the review say about hard seasons?", "o": ["Strong faith avoids all hard seasons", "Rooted faith stays alive in hard seasons", "Hard seasons mean God left", "We should hide in hard seasons"], "a": 1, "e": "Rooted faith still faces heat and drought, but stays alive because it is connected to the source."}], "action": "Text or call one person today and share one thing you learned this week. Faith that is shared grows deeper roots.", "journal": "Of the six roots from this chapter, which one did you need most this week? Why?", "closing": {"t": "rooted and built up in him, and established in the faith, even as you were taught, abounding in it in thanksgiving.", "r": "Colossians 2:7"}}}, "prompts": ["Where did you notice God’s presence today, even in a small way?", "What worry do you need to lay down today?", "What are three things you are grateful for right now?", "In which area of your life do you need to trust God more?", "What would living fully in your calling look like this week?", "Who could you encourage today, and how?", "What is God teaching you in this season?", "Is there someone you need to forgive? What makes it hard?", "What small step can you take toward God tomorrow?", "How can you glorify God with your plans for tomorrow?"], "verses": [{"t": "Your word is a lamp to my feet, and a light for my path.", "r": "Psalm 119:105"}, {"t": "Don’t you be afraid, for I am with you. Don’t be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you. Yes, I will help you. Yes, I will uphold you with the right hand of my righteousness.", "r": "Isaiah 41:10"}, {"t": "In nothing be anxious, but in everything, by prayer and petition with thanksgiving, let your requests be made known to God.", "r": "Philippians 4:6"}, {"t": "“Be still, and know that I am God. I will be exalted among the nations. I will be exalted in the earth.”", "r": "Psalm 46:10"}, {"t": "“Come to me, all you who labor and are heavily burdened, and I will give you rest.", "r": "Matthew 11:28"}, {"t": "Trust in Yahweh with all your heart, and don’t lean on your own understanding.", "r": "Proverbs 3:5"}, {"t": "We know that all things work together for good for those who love God, for those who are called according to his purpose.", "r": "Romans 8:28"}, {"t": "Haven’t I commanded you? Be strong and courageous. Don’t be afraid. Don’t be dismayed, for Yahweh your God is with you wherever you go.”", "r": "Joshua 1:9"}, {"t": "For I know the thoughts that I think toward you,” says Yahweh, “thoughts of peace, and not of evil, to give you hope and a future.", "r": "Jeremiah 29:11"}, {"t": "Yahweh is my shepherd: I shall lack nothing.", "r": "Psalm 23:1"}, {"t": "but those who wait for Yahweh will renew their strength. They will mount up with wings like eagles. They will run, and not be weary. They will walk, and not faint.", "r": "Isaiah 40:31"}, {"t": "Therefore if anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation. The old things have passed away. Behold, all things have become new.", "r": "2 Corinthians 5:17"}, {"t": "It is because of Yahweh’s loving kindnesses that we are not consumed, because his compassion doesn’t fail.", "r": "Lamentations 3:22"}, {"t": "Yahweh is my light and my salvation. Whom shall I fear? Yahweh is the strength of my life. Of whom shall I be afraid?", "r": "Psalm 27:1"}, {"t": "Peace I leave with you. My peace I give to you; not as the world gives, I give to you. Don’t let your heart be troubled, neither let it be fearful.", "r": "John 14:27"}, {"t": "Now faith is assurance of things hoped for, proof of things not seen.", "r": "Hebrews 11:1"}, {"t": "casting all your worries on him, because he cares for you.", "r": "1 Peter 5:7"}, {"t": "Yahweh is near to those who have a broken heart, and saves those who have a crushed spirit.", "r": "Psalm 34:18"}, {"t": "Now may the God of hope fill you with all joy and peace in believing, that you may abound in hope, in the power of the Holy Spirit.", "r": "Romans 15:13"}, {"t": "Let’s not be weary in doing good, for we will reap in due season, if we don’t give up.", "r": "Galatians 6:9"}, {"t": "Commit your way to Yahweh. Trust also in him, and he will do this:", "r": "Psalm 37:5"}, {"t": "Therefore don’t be anxious for tomorrow, for tomorrow will be anxious for itself. Each day’s own evil is sufficient.", "r": "Matthew 6:34"}, {"t": "I can do all things through Christ, who strengthens me.", "r": "Philippians 4:13"}, {"t": "I have told you these things, that in me you may have peace. In the world you have trouble; but cheer up! I have overcome the world.”", "r": "John 16:33"}, {"t": "Yahweh, your God, is among you, a mighty one who will save. He will rejoice over you with joy. He will calm you in his love. He will rejoice over you with singing.", "r": "Zephaniah 3:17"}, {"t": "He who dwells in the secret place of the Most High will rest in the shadow of the Almighty.", "r": "Psalm 91:1"}, {"t": "But if any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask of God, who gives to all liberally and without reproach, and it will be given to him.", "r": "James 1:5"}, {"t": "He has shown you, O man, what is good. What does Yahweh require of you, but to act justly, to love mercy, and to walk humbly with your God?", "r": "Micah 6:8"}, {"t": "And whatever you do, work heartily, as for the Lord, and not for men,", "r": "Colossians 3:23"}, {"t": "My help comes from Yahweh, who made heaven and earth.", "r": "Psalm 121:2"}, {"t": "You will keep whoever’s mind is steadfast in perfect peace, because he trusts in you.", "r": "Isaiah 26:3"}]};